Z||000007||BENGAL4||10-20-1999||03:02 PM||||With the 4 cyl, you just have gears and no chain and you have to drop the engine in order to get the cam out. There are some posts in the forum regarding this procedure. Think positive that its only the pick up coil. Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by BENGAL4 (edited 10-20-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||SloPonti||10-20-1999||07:48 PM||||well i check to see if the rotor was turning when i crank the engine and it is not! that really really sucks! so now i need to know if is the cam gear or the timing gears! either way i think i have to pull the timing gear cover for both operations! does anyone have any hints or suggestions for doing this!<BR>thanks<BR>Rob||||reg||13
Z||000009||theogre||10-20-1999||08:28 PM||||there are a couple ways.<P>1. the "GM specified" method is to pull the cam out. The gear is pressed on. This can be done with the motor in the car if you drop the back of the cradle.<P>2. use a puller kit. On such kit is available here, <A HREF="http://www.hands-on.org/pfkb/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hands-on.org/pfkb/</A> <BR>I have not done any business with these people and don't know anyone that's tryed the kit. It looks like it should work, but I have no way to know.<P>Also, just to make sure it's the cam gears, take out the distributor and check the drive gear. This may just be a waste of time but it's a hell of allot less time than messing with the cam. It's an 84 so it's likely the timing set is fried, but pull the dist as your first step.||||reg||1
Z||000010||SloPonti||10-21-1999||07:29 PM||||ok today i droped the right side cradle and pulled off the timing cover and the big gear on the end of the cam(timing gear) is really chewed up! i talked to a ex gm tech(now a shop teacher in the auto program i am in) said that when i go to put the new one on i need to heat up the gear a little and freeze the cam shaft with c02! but i do not have c02 or access to this! does anyone know what i can use to get the new gear onto the camshaft?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>thanks<BR>Rob||||reg||5
Z||000011||fierospeeder||10-21-1999||09:28 PM||||check the resistance on the coil, if its fine, i had problems with my moms 2.8l engine in a celebrity, i replaced the coil and after a few days two wires wouldn't fire, i checked the resistance and it was fine and i replaced the ignition module and now it runs fine,  try to check things out wtih a multimeter, the electronics get expensive after replacing stuff you think is wrong.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||RZURCTN||10-21-1999||09:59 PM||||SLOPONTI,  tell your shop teacher to take a long walk off a short pier!  I just performed this in my 84SE.  I lowered both sides of the rear cradle. Pull out the distributor and the oil pump gear(located right next to the oil filter.  Remove your push rods and lifters(don't get them mixed up, put them somewhere safe and be sure they are labled for later return to the proper position.)  Unbolt the thrust plate from the block(located behind the camshaft gear and accessed through the two holes in the gear.  Carefully remove the camshaft, you don't want to scrape it on any of the bearings.  The gear comes off easily in a shop press.  Be very careful of the thrust plate, they will shatter if bent just a little!  A new timing kit costed me about $17.00 at Advanced auto.  I didn't have to heat or freeze anything!  The new gear presses on nice and smooth too. Be sure to check the clearances for the thrust plate and spacer, replace if necessary.  I had no problems changing mine, good luck!!!  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||SloPonti||10-22-1999||12:09 AM||||why would i want to take anything more out than i absoluty have to! the gm guy said they used to perform this operation on the car and so far his answers have worked-never do more work than you have to! thanks<BR>Rob||||reg||1
